  'quant-03-broadcasting-basics',
  E'Broadcasting basics',
  E'Your strategy holds 4 positions on each of 3 days. You want to subtract the risk-free rate (a single 3-day vector) from every row of returns. Two for-loops? No. numpy''s broadcasting handles it as one expression — adds about a microsecond, regardless of how many positions you have.',
  E'Read a broadcast subtraction and predict the resulting first row.',
  E'**Concept.** When a `(3,)` vector meets a `(4, 3)` matrix, numpy implicitly stretches the vector along the missing axis — as if you''d repeated it four times. Shapes must align from the *trailing* axis. The big win is no temporary copies — the C loop just reuses the smaller buffer.\n\n**Example.**\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n# 4 positions × 3 days of raw daily returns.\nraw = np.full((4, 3), 0.012)\n# Risk-free rate per day (3-vector).\nrf = np.array([0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0002])\nexcess = raw - rf\nprint(excess[0])\n```\n\n**Predict.** Predict the first row of `excess` — the 4-position raw returns minus the 3-day risk-free vector.\n\n**Expected.** `[0.0119 0.0119 0.0118]`',
  null,
  null,
